        <section class="capability-strip" aria-label="Compiler capabilities"><span>C#-style syntax</span><span class="strip-plus">+</span><span>Native compilation</span><span class="strip-plus">+</span><span>JIT execution</span><span class="strip-plus">+</span><span>Extensible targets</span></section>

        <section id="philosophy" class="section philosophy">
            <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>01 /</span> LESS BETWEEN YOU AND THE MACHINE</p><h2>High-level thinking.<br /><span>Low-level freedom.</span></h2></div><p>Write in a language that feels like home.<br />Build something that stands on its own.</p></div>
            <div class="feature-grid">
                <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">{<span> </span>}</div><span class="card-index">01</span><h3>Familiar by nature.</h3><p>Classes, interfaces, namespaces, and expressive C#-style syntax. A growing language surface for ideas you already know how to write.</p><div class="card-foot"><span>C#-STYLE LANGUAGE</span><span>↗</span></div></article>
                <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon chip-icon">▧</div><span class="card-index">02</span><h3>Native at its core.</h3><p>Go straight from source to machine code. Standalone native executables without a .NET runtime, VM, or garbage collector.</p><div class="card-foot"><span>DIRECT TO THE MACHINE</span><span>↗</span></div></article>
                <article class="feature-card"><div class="feature-icon">⌘</div><span class="card-index">03</span><h3>Open by architecture.</h3><p>A handwritten frontend. A defined intermediate representation. Pluggable target backends that keep the compiler open to new possibilities.</p><div class="card-foot"><span>BUILT TO BE EXTENDED</span><span>↗</span></div></article>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="benchmarks" class="section benchmarks" aria-labelledby="benchmark-title">
            <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>02 /</span> SMALL PROGRAM. REAL NUMBERS.</p><h2 id="benchmark-title">A lighter footprint.<br /><span>Right out of the compiler.</span></h2></div><p>The same hello. Three different compilers.<br />A focused look at the cost of a tiny native program.</p></div>
            <div class="benchmark-layout">
                <div class="benchmark-summary"><div class="benchmark-kicker"><span class="status-dot"></span> HELLO-WORLD SNAPSHOT (RESULTS AVERAGE OF 5)</div><div class="benchmark-highlight">@benchmarks[selectedBenchmark].Highlight<span>@benchmarks[selectedBenchmark].Suffix</span></div><h3>@benchmarks[selectedBenchmark].Headline</h3><p>@benchmarks[selectedBenchmark].Description</p><div class="benchmark-machine"><span>TEST ENVIRONMENT</span><strong>Linux · x86-64 dev VM</strong><span>WORKLOAD</span><strong>Print “hello”, no newline</strong></div></div>
                <div class="benchmark-chart"><div class="benchmark-toolbar"><div class="benchmark-switch" role="group" aria-label="Benchmark metric">@for(var i = 0; i < benchmarks.Length; i++) { var metric = i; <button class="@(selectedBenchmark == i ? "active" : "")" aria-pressed="@((selectedBenchmark == i).ToString().ToLowerInvariant())" @onclick="() => selectedBenchmark = metric">@benchmarks[i].Name</button> }</div><span class="lower-better">↓ LOWER IS BETTER</span></div>
                    <div class="benchmark-rows" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" @key="selectedBenchmark">
                        @foreach(var row in benchmarks[selectedBenchmark].Rows) { <div class="benchmark-row @(row.Label == "RubyC" ? "ruby-row" : "")"><div class="benchmark-row-label"><span>@row.Label <small>@row.Detail</small></span><strong>@row.Display</strong></div><div class="benchmark-track" aria-hidden="true">@if(row.Width > 0) { <div class="benchmark-bar" style="width: @(row.Width)%"><span></span></div> } else { <div class="benchmark-missing">NO MEASUREMENT REPORTED</div> }</div></div> }
                    </div><div class="benchmark-chart-foot"><span>WORKLOAD: HELLO WORLD</span><span>@benchmarks[selectedBenchmark].Unit</span></div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <details class="benchmark-method"><summary><span>Behind the numbers</span><span>Methodology &amp; build commands <b aria-hidden="true">+</b></span></summary><div class="method-content"><div><h3>A documented snapshot, not a universal ranking.</h3><p>Measurements are reproduced from the RubyC project README, not rerun by this website. Runtime is a 200-run average on the x86-64 development VM and includes process spawn. Small timings vary between runs and machines.</p><p>RubyC’s binary is fully static with no dependencies. C and C++ use the default dynamically linked GCC/G++ <code>-O2</code> builds. These are different linking configurations; results describe this hello-world example, not general application performance. C++ compile time was not reported.</p></div><pre><code># Equivalent programs printing hello
gcc -O2 hello.c -o hello_c
g++ -O2 hello.cpp -o hello_cpp
rubyc build hello.rc --target linux_x86_64 -o hello_rc</code></pre></div></details>
            <p class="benchmark-caption">Source: RubyC README · hello-world comparison · static RubyC vs. dynamically linked C / C++.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="playground" class="section playground">
            <div class="playground-copy"><p class="eyebrow"><span>03 /</span> FROM IDEA TO EXECUTABLE</p><h2>Looks familiar.<br /><span>Goes further.</span></h2><p>A little source. A native result.<br />Explore the shape of RubyC, from your first hello to a standalone build.</p><div class="example-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Code examples">@for (var i = 0; i < examples.Length; i++) { var index = i; <button id="tab-@i" role="tab" aria-selected="@((selectedExample == i).ToString().ToLowerInvariant())" aria-controls="example-panel" tabindex="@(selectedExample == i ? 0 : -1)" @onkeydown="HandleTabKey" class="@(selectedExample == i ? "active" : "")" @onclick="() => SelectExample(index)"><span>0@(i + 1)</span> @examples[i].Title <span class="tab-arrow">↗</span></button> }</div><p class="example-note">An illustrative preview. Run RubyC locally to compile.</p></div>
            <div class="editor"><div class="editor-titlebar"><div class="window-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><span>@examples[selectedExample].File</span><span class="editor-language">RubyC</span></div><div id="example-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-@selectedExample" tabindex="0" class="code-panel"><pre>@((MarkupString)examples[selectedExample].Html)</pre></div><div class="terminal"><div class="terminal-heading"><span>TERMINAL</span><button @onclick="CopyCommand" aria-label="Copy build command">@(copied ? "Copied ✓" : "Copy command ⧉")</button></div><div class="terminal-command"><span>❯</span> <code>@examples[selectedExample].Command</code></div><div class="terminal-output" aria-live="polite"><span>↳</span> @examples[selectedExample].Output</div></div><div class="editor-status"><span><span class="status-dot"></span> @examples[selectedExample].Mode</span><span>linux_x86_64</span></div></div>
        </section>

        <section id="architecture" class="section architecture"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span>04 /</span> ENGINEERED FROM FIRST PRINCIPLES</p><h2>No black box.<br /><span>Just a clear path to native.</span></h2></div><p>From a handwritten parser to an x86-64 backend,<br />every stage has a purpose. Every layer is yours to explore.</p></div><div class="pipeline"><div class="pipeline-node"><span class="node-number">01</span><strong>Source</strong><code>.rc / C#-style</code></div><div class="pipeline-connector"><span></span>→</div><div class="pipeline-node"><span class="node-number">02</span><strong>Frontend</strong><code>Parse · analyse</code></div><div class="pipeline-connector"><span></span>→</div><div class="pipeline-node ir-node"><span class="node-number">03</span><strong>RubyC IR</strong><code>The common language</code></div><div class="pipeline-connector"><span></span>→</div><div class="pipeline-node"><span class="node-number">04</span><strong>Target</strong><code>Lower · generate</code></div><div class="pipeline-connector"><span></span>→</div><div class="pipeline-node"><span class="node-number">05</span><strong>Native</strong><code>Build / JIT</code></div></div><div class="architecture-details"><p><span>↳</span> Built-in language frontends. Pluggable OS targets.</p><span class="architecture-tag">CURRENT BACKEND <strong>Linux x86-64</strong></span></div></section>

        <section id="status" class="section status-section"><div class="status-copy"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span> ACTIVELY EVOLVING</p><h2>Small beginnings.<br /><span>Native ambitions.</span></h2><p>RubyC is an independent compiler in active development. Native output is the foundation; expanding C# language support and real-world library compatibility is the next frontier.</p><div class="status-notice-card"><span class="notice-badge">PROJECT STATUS</span><p>RubyC is currently under construction. The source code is being uploaded shortly to Git for RubyC, and only Linux files are being compiled at the moment.</p></div><p class="status-note">An early-stage project, with a growing C# subset.<br />Full .NET compatibility is not yet available.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/rubyc-project" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Follow the development <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="status-roadmap"><div><span class="roadmap-marker complete">✓</span><p><span>THE FOUNDATION</span><strong>Source to native. End to end.</strong><small>Frontend, IR, native code generation, and JIT.</small></p></div><div><span class="roadmap-marker current"></span><p><span>IN DEVELOPMENT</span><strong>A wider world of C#.</strong><small>Language coverage and library compatibility.</small></p></div><div><span class="roadmap-marker future">+</span><p><span>THE HORIZON</span><strong>More targets. More possibilities.</strong><small>An extensible architecture to build upon.</small></p></div></div></section>

        <section id="contribute" class="contribute-section" aria-labelledby="contribute-title"><div class="contribute-symbol" aria-hidden="true">&lt;/&gt;</div><div><p class="eyebrow">AN OPEN INVITATION</p><h2 id="contribute-title">Build RubyC with us.</h2><p>Curious about compilers? There’s a place for your ideas.<br />Explore the code, share feedback, and help shape what comes next.</p><div class="contribution-areas"><span>Compiler engineering</span><span>Testing</span><span>Documentation</span></div></div><a class="button button-outline" href="https://github.com/rubyc-project" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get involved on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>

